#!/usr/bin/env bash
# Can a wheel hook over a thin obstacle and hang there? Measure it.
#
#   bin/hook              # every mode, in the order the question has to be asked
#   bin/hook hang         # does a PLACED hang hold, per obstacle shape
#   bin/hook catch        # can an arriving bike CATCH a tip, and how often
#   bin/hook ride         # the entry a player would attempt: off the end of a deck
#   bin/hook reach        # what a hang can collect, and what gets you out of one
#
# WHY THIS EXISTS. The idea, from the user and recalling Elasto Mania: "hooking
# one tire over a thing and sliding — in Elastomania it would stretch your bike
# out", with the apples underneath the poles so the mechanic teaches itself.
# Geometry said yes: the chassis colliders do not ground out at any nose-down
# pitch. Geometry is not dynamics, and this is the dynamics.
#
# THE ANSWER IS NO, and the rig is kept so that "no" stays checkable rather than
# being a paragraph somebody has to trust. See "Hooking a wheel, and why it does
# not work" in the README for the numbers this prints and what they mean.
#
# The short version:
#   * a wheel is a CIRCLE, so it cannot hook anything. On a flat lip the contact
#     point slides along as the wheel rolls and nothing resists the moment of a
#     body hanging off one side, so it rolls off, every time;
#   * only a razor-sharp blade tip holds, because a VERTEX pins the contact
#     point — and once you are on one it holds indefinitely, with no input;
#   * but the catch window is about 1% of plausible arrivals and closes entirely
#     above walking pace, and a ridden run-off never catches for longer than a
#     quarter of a second;
#   * and every catch that holds bottoms the suspension, so the stretch cannot
#     be a readout of how hard you came in.
#
# It takes a couple of minutes: the sweeps are tens of thousands of short sims.
source "$(cd "$(dirname "${BASH_SOURCE[0]}")" && pwd)/_common.sh"
ensure_deps
cd "$ROOT"
exec "$ROOT/node_modules/.bin/tsx" "$ROOT/bin/_hook-main.ts" "$@"
